  "classification": {
    "baselineMasks": "Every original 400-pixel mask SHA-256 reproduced exactly.",
    "sourceTracking": "Track original feature pixels through the crop homography into source coordinates, then through each perturbed inverse.",
    "variants": "Original plus translations and radial corner displacements of +/-0.25, +/-0.5 and +/-1 source pixels; 200, 400 and 600 output pixels.",
    "arms": "Re-estimate production Lab clusters, and separately freeze the original cluster centers."
  },
  "findings": [
    "Both failed bottom patches are excluded at 200px because their nearest pixel is one cell from the boundary. The same fixed crop has a two-cell gap at 400px and a three-cell gap at 600px, making both mandatory. All three masks otherwise satisfy the area/core cutoffs. This isolates the fixed rectified-pixel boundary rule as one cause of resolution-dependent requirements.",
    "For the outline star at 400px, +0.25px horizontal or -0.25px vertical source-corner translation changes the patch from mandatory to excluded. Its classified pixels mostly remain; a gate changes, not the underlying photographed motif.",
    "The star interior, bird eye, bird body opening and Viking opening are mandatory in all 57 variants per classifier arm. These are controls, not proof that every legitimate near-boundary motif can be distinguished.",
    "Re-estimating versus freezing the color clusters has identical mandatory counts for the two failed fragments. Sampling and the boundary criterion explain these switches without a color-cluster change.",
    "The bird also has an unstable bottom fragment. Its real eye has relatively low classifier margin, so a simple low-confidence threshold would risk deleting genuine details."
  ],
  "interpretation": "The two saved reconstruction failures include photo-border evidence instability. This does not establish exact paper identity in the blurred band or prove that either exported template is correct. It also does not explain the timed Viking solver failure, whose opening is stable across all variants.",
  "next": "Evaluate source-resolution-aware photographic uncertainty with preserved import provenance, including true near-boundary controls. Keep all original image-error pixels and drawn-mask features. Do not silently drop the existing checks or reclassify benchmark failures.",
